export default "---\ntitle: 'PCA as a Compression Contract: What Variance Preserves and What It Misses'\ndate: '2024-09-22'\nslug: 'pca-compression-contract'\ntags: ['Machine Learning', 'PCA', 'Dimensionality Reduction', 'Python']\ncategory: 'ML & Deep Learning'\nexcerpt: 'A geometric and practical guide to PCA: derive the components, compress correlated features, choose dimensions, and spot failure modes in practice.'\nstatus: 'draft'\n---\n\n![PCA animation: an axis rotates through candidate directions until it locks onto PC₁ — the direction of maximum variance — then each data point projects onto it with minimal residual](../Assets/blogs/pca-animation.mp4)\n\n_An axis searches all orientations for maximum variance, locks onto PC₁ (96.2 %), then projects each point — the dashed residuals show what is discarded when the second dimension is dropped._\n\n![PCA pipeline: correlated measurements → center → rotate → keep top k → smaller representation](../Assets/blogs/pca-pipeline-overview.png)\n\n_PCA searches for the directions in which data spreads the most, keeps those directions, and measures how much is left behind._\n\nPCA does not delete columns. It builds new directions from the original columns, ranks them by how much variation they carry, and lets you keep only the most informative ones.\n\n## PCA in one sentence\n\nPrincipal Component Analysis, or PCA, compresses a dataset by finding the directions in which its observations differ the most.\n\nThat sentence contains the whole idea. The rest of this article explains why those directions are useful, how PCA finds them, and when \"most variation\" is the wrong definition of importance.\n\nThis article has two layers:\n\n- **Layer 1 builds the intuition.** No linear algebra is required.\n- **Layer 2 derives the mathematics and implementation.** It is optional, but useful if you want to implement or defend PCA technically.\n\n## Layer 1: Build the intuition\n\n### Why too many features becomes a problem\n\nBefore getting into what PCA does, it is worth asking why we need it at all.\n\nSuppose you are building a model to predict house prices and you start adding features: number of rooms, number of bathrooms, square footage, distance to the nearest school, number of nearby grocery shops, age of the building, and so on. Up to a point, more features help. But beyond some optimal number, adding more features actually hurts — the model becomes more complex, training slows down, and generalization gets worse. This is the curse of dimensionality.\n\nThe solution is dimensionality reduction: reduce the number of features without losing the information that matters. Two broad approaches exist.\n\n**Feature selection** keeps a subset of the original columns and discards the rest. It does not create anything new.\n\n**Feature extraction** creates new columns by combining the original ones, then keeps only the most informative combinations. PCA is the most widely used feature extraction technique.\n\n### The house example: when feature selection is not enough\n\nStart with a simple dataset — houses described by two inputs: number of rooms and number of nearby grocery shops.\n\nIf you plot these two features against each other, rooms vary widely while grocery shop counts barely change across the dataset. The data spreads mostly along the rooms axis. If you had to keep only one column, you would keep rooms — that is where the information lives.\n\nThat decision is straightforward feature selection: look at each column's spread, pick the one with more variance.\n\nNow change the second column. Instead of grocery shops, use number of bathrooms.\n\nThe picture changes. Rooms and bathrooms are correlated — a larger flat has more of both. The data now spreads diagonally. Both columns carry comparable variance. Dropping either one throws away real information about house size.\n\nA real estate expert would say: don't drop either column. Instead, combine them. What you really want to know is the overall size of the flat — one number that captures both rooms and bathrooms together. A large flat has many rooms _and_ many bathrooms; the combination tells you more than either feature alone.\n\nPCA does this algorithmically. It does not pick existing columns — it constructs new ones that are the most informative linear combinations of the originals.\n\n### The photographer analogy: what PCA is really doing\n\nThink of a football match happening in a 3D stadium. A photographer needs to capture it in a 2D photograph for a newspaper. The match is 3D but the photo is flat, so the photographer must choose an angle.\n\nStand in the wrong spot and all the players look clustered on top of each other — you cannot tell them apart. Walk around the stadium until you find the angle where the players spread out the most across the frame. That is the shot.\n\nPCA is that photographer. Your dataset lives in high-dimensional space (many features). PCA walks around looking for the projection direction where the data points spread out the most. That direction becomes PC1. The next most informative direction, perpendicular to the first, becomes PC2. You keep the top few directions and discard the rest.\n\nThe key word is _spread_. More spread means data points stay distinguishable after the projection. Less spread means they collapse together and the model cannot tell them apart.\n\n### Why variance is the right measure of spread\n\nHere is the concrete reason variance matters in PCA — not as a formula, but as a geometric argument.\n\nConsider two data points: one belonging to class A, one to class B. In two dimensions they are clearly separated. Now you need to project them onto a single axis to reduce dimensions.\n\nIf you project onto the axis of low variance (the direction where the data barely changes), the two points land nearly on top of each other. Your ML model sees almost identical values and cannot distinguish the classes. The compression destroyed the separation.\n\nIf you project onto the axis of high variance (the direction where the data spreads the most), the two points remain well separated. The ML model can still tell them apart.\n\nThis is why PCA maximizes variance: projecting onto the high-variance direction preserves the distances between points as much as possible. Distances between points encode the structure your model needs to learn.\n\nMean absolute deviation would measure a similar thing, but it is not differentiable at zero — you cannot run gradient-based optimization through it. Variance is differentiable everywhere, which is why it appears in PCA's objective rather than simpler spread measures.\n\n### The concrete worked example\n\nTo ground this in numbers, here are four readings from two vibration sensors on the same machine.\n\n| Reading | Sensor A | Sensor B |\n| ------: | -------: | -------: |\n|       1 |        2 |        1 |\n|       2 |        3 |        2 |\n|       3 |        4 |        2 |\n|       4 |        5 |        4 |\n\n![Scatter plot of the four sensor readings and their feature mean](../Assets/blogs/PCA%20Step%201%20Original%20Space.png)\n\n_The four readings form a mostly diagonal pattern — sensors A and B tend to rise together, so the data spreads along a diagonal rather than filling the square evenly._\n\nThe sensors are correlated. Like rooms and bathrooms, they share an underlying signal. PCA's job is to find the direction along which this shared variation runs and compress both sensors into one coordinate.\n\n### A small vocabulary before going further\n\n| Term                | Plain-language meaning                                                                 |\n| ------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Feature             | One input column                                                                       |\n| Dimension           | One direction needed to describe an observation; often one original feature before PCA |\n| Centering           | Subtracting each feature's average so the data is arranged around zero                 |\n| Variance            | How spread out the values are along one direction                                      |\n| Covariance          | Whether two features tend to move together                                             |\n| Principal component | A new direction formed as a linear combination of the original features                |\n| Projection          | The coordinate of an observation along a chosen direction                              |\n| Residual            | The part of an observation the chosen components cannot retain                         |\n\n### What PCA does to the coordinate system\n\nPCA rotates the coordinate axes. The data points do not move relative to one another — we change the axes used to describe them.\n\nFor the sensor data, PCA finds two new axes:\n\n$$\\text{PC1} = 0.717 \\times \\text{Sensor A} + 0.697 \\times \\text{Sensor B}$$\n\n$$\\text{PC2} = 0.697 \\times \\text{Sensor A} - 0.717 \\times \\text{Sensor B}$$\n\nThe weights in PC1 are nearly equal, so PC1 behaves like a balanced summary of both sensors — analogous to the \"size of flat\" in the house example. PC2 represents the difference between them: how much one sensor disagrees with the other.\n\nYou get exactly as many principal components as you have original features. Two original columns → two principal components. The first PC points in the direction of maximum variance; each subsequent PC points in the next most informative direction perpendicular to all previous ones.\n\nCompression happens when you keep only the top k components and discard the rest.\n\n### Why centering is the first step\n\nBefore finding those directions, PCA subtracts the mean from every feature. The two sensor averages are:\n\n```text\nSensor A mean = 3.50\nSensor B mean = 2.25\n```\n\nSubtract those averages from every reading:\n\n```text\n[-1.50, -1.25]\n[-0.50, -0.25]\n[ 0.50, -0.25]\n[ 1.50,  1.75]\n```\n\nCentering moves the cloud of points to be arranged around zero. Moving the entire cloud from one location to another should not change its internal shape or the directions of maximum spread. Without centering, PCA would mix up the cloud's position in space with its actual shape.\n\n![Centered sensor readings with PC1 and PC2 drawn as perpendicular axes](../Assets/blogs/PCA%20Step%202%20Principal%20Axes.png)\n\n_After centering, PC1 points along the long diagonal of the cloud — the direction of maximum spread. PC2 runs perpendicular, crossing the narrow width. This rotation is what PCA calls its coordinate transformation._\n\n### Projection: turning a point into one coordinate\n\nOnce PCA has PC1, it projects every data point onto that direction. Each reading is replaced by a single number: its position along PC1.\n\nThe first reading `(2, 1)` becomes approximately `-1.947` on PC1. The negative sign means it lies on the below-average side. A different software library might return `+1.947` by flipping the PC1 direction — both representations carry the same information.\n\nPCA can also invert the projection to approximate the original space. The reconstruction of `(2, 1)` is approximately `(2.104, 0.893)` — close but not exact, because PC2 was discarded and the small disagreement between the sensors is gone.\n\n![Centered readings projected onto PC1 with residual distances](../Assets/blogs/PCA%20Step%203%20Projection%20for%20RAG%20Blog.png)\n\n_The green diamonds are the compressed positions on PC1. The orange segments show the perpendicular distance lost by dropping PC2 — this is the residual that the single component cannot retain._\n\n## What \"explained variance\" actually means\n\nThe two components divide the total spread of the sensor data:\n\n```text\nPC1: 96.2%\nPC2:  3.8%\n```\n\n![One-component reconstruction beside the explained-variance split](../Assets/blogs/PCA%20Step%204%20Reconstruction%20and%20Variance.png)\n\n_The reconstructed points stay close to the originals because PC1 captures 96.2% of the variation. The remaining gaps are the contribution of PC2 — the sensor disagreement that a one-component representation cannot preserve._\n\nThis is called the explained-variance ratio. It tells you how much of the dataset's total variation lies along each component.\n\nThe phrase is a bit misleading. \"Explained\" sounds like \"understood\" or \"useful.\" PCA has not explained why the machine vibrates — it found a direction that captures most of the numerical spread. Whether that spread is meaningful is a separate question entirely.\n\nHigh variance is not automatically important. Lighting changes may dominate image pixels without helping a classifier. A subtle change in a medical signal may matter far more than a large swing in ambient temperature. PCA never sees the prediction target or the cost of an error.\n\n## How many components should we keep?\n\nIf a dataset starts with 100 features, PCA can create as many as 100 components. Dimensionality reduction happens when we keep only the first `k` components and discard the rest.\n\nA cumulative explained-variance curve shows how much variation is retained as `k` grows. Choosing enough components to retain 90–95% is a common starting point, not a universal rule. The right answer depends on what you are trying to do.\n\n| Goal          | What should be checked?                                            |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Visualization | Do clusters, neighborhoods, and outliers still make sense?         |\n| Compression   | Is the storage reduction worth the reconstruction error?           |\n| Denoising     | Do the removed directions behave like noise on new data?           |\n| Prediction    | Does the reduced representation preserve the held-out task metric? |\n\nFor the sensor example, keeping PC1 is reasonable for general compression. But if sensor disagreement is an early fault signal, dropping PC2 deletes exactly the information you needed.\n\n## Scaling changes the meaning of \"largest variation\"\n\nThe two sensors use the same unit and similar numeric ranges, so their raw variances can be compared directly.\n\nNow add a power measurement in watts. Power values may be hundreds of times larger than vibration values simply because of the unit chosen. PCA sees that large numeric spread and may let power dominate PC1 — not because power is more informative, but because it has a bigger number.\n\nStandardization fixes this by dividing each feature by its standard deviation, so every feature starts with unit variance before PCA runs.\n\nIt is not an automatic cleanup step. Standardization changes the question PCA is answering:\n\n- Without scaling: which physical measurements vary the most?\n- With scaling: which relationships matter when every feature starts from an equal footing?\n\nScikit-learn's [`PCA`](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.decomposition.PCA.html) centers dense data but does not scale each feature. [`StandardScaler`](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.preprocessing.StandardScaler.html) performs the separate scaling step.\n\n![Scaling decision flowchart: comparable units skip StandardScaler, otherwise fit scaler on training fold only, then fit PCA](../Assets/blogs/pca-scaling-decision.png)\n\n_A production PCA pipeline: decide on scaling, fit both transformers inside the training fold only — never on the full dataset._\n\nPCA belongs inside the training pipeline. Both scaling and PCA learn parameters from data — the feature means, standard deviations, and principal directions. Fitting them on the full dataset before a train-test split leaks evaluation information into the model.\n\nA scikit-learn [`Pipeline`](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/compose.html) keeps the transformations inside each cross-validation fold automatically.\n\n## Where PCA works—and where it does not\n\nPCA is a good candidate when continuous features are correlated and the useful structure is approximately linear. It can reduce storage, speed up distance calculations, remove small noisy directions, and produce exploratory two- or three-dimensional visualizations.\n\nIts objective also creates predictable failure modes.\n\n**A low-variance signal can still matter.** PCA may discard a direction that carries rare but predictive information — the exact problem from the sensor disagreement example above.\n\n**Curved structure cannot be unfolded by a linear rotation.** Spirals, rings, and curved surfaces need nonlinear methods like UMAP or kernel PCA.\n\n**Outliers can rotate the components.** Large deviations have an outsized effect on variance and covariance, pulling PC1 toward themselves.\n\n**Components mix original features.** Each principal component is a weighted combination of all original features, which reduces interpretability compared to simply keeping the original columns.\n\n**The learned directions can drift.** A new operating regime — a different product mix, a shifted sensor, a seasonal pattern — may have a different covariance structure. Components learned on old data may not be the most informative ones for new data.\n\nIf a requirement is not represented by variance, PCA has no reason to preserve it.\n\nAt this point, a general reader has the complete practical picture. The next two sections derive why eigenvectors appear and show a full implementation.\n\n## Layer 2: Optional mathematics—why eigenvectors appear\n\n### The covariance matrix captures spread and orientation together\n\nVariance measures spread along one axis at a time. But PCA needs to understand how the data is spread across all pairs of features simultaneously.\n\nThe covariance matrix does this. For a two-feature dataset with columns $X$ and $Y$, the covariance matrix is:\n\n$$C = \\begin{bmatrix} \\text{Var}(X) & \\text{Cov}(X,Y) \\\\ \\text{Cov}(X,Y) & \\text{Var}(Y) \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nThe diagonal entries are the variances of each feature individually. The off-diagonal entries are covariances: positive means the two features tend to rise together; negative means one rises as the other falls.\n\nThe covariance matrix is symmetric — $\\text{Cov}(X,Y) = \\text{Cov}(Y,X)$. This symmetry turns out to be important: symmetric matrices always have real eigenvalues and their eigenvectors can always be chosen to be perpendicular, which is exactly the orthogonality property PCA relies on.\n\n### From optimization problem to eigenvector equation\n\nLet a centered observation be the vector $\\mathbf{x}$. Choose a unit-length direction $\\mathbf{w}$. The coordinate of $\\mathbf{x}$ along that direction is:\n\n$$z = \\mathbf{w}^\\top \\mathbf{x}$$\n\nThe unit-length constraint $\\mathbf{w}^\\top \\mathbf{w} = 1$ prevents manufacturing fake variance by scaling $\\mathbf{w}$ up arbitrarily.\n\nFor $n$ centered observations, the sample variance of the projected coordinates is:\n\n$$\\text{Var}(z) = \\frac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} z_i^2$$\n\nSubstituting $z_i = \\mathbf{w}^\\top \\mathbf{x}_i$ and rearranging:\n\n$$\\text{Var}(z) = \\mathbf{w}^\\top \\left[\\frac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{x}_i \\mathbf{x}_i^\\top\\right] \\mathbf{w}$$\n\nThe bracketed matrix is exactly the covariance matrix $C$. So the problem becomes:\n\n$$\\underset{\\mathbf{w}}{\\text{maximize}} \\;\\; \\mathbf{w}^\\top C \\mathbf{w} \\quad \\text{subject to} \\quad \\mathbf{w}^\\top \\mathbf{w} = 1$$\n\nUse a Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda$ to enforce the constraint:\n\n$$\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbf{w}, \\lambda) = \\mathbf{w}^\\top C \\mathbf{w} - \\lambda\\,(\\mathbf{w}^\\top \\mathbf{w} - 1)$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $\\mathbf{w}$ and set to zero:\n\n$$2C\\mathbf{w} - 2\\lambda\\mathbf{w} = 0 \\implies C\\mathbf{w} = \\lambda\\mathbf{w}$$\n\nThis is the eigenvector equation. It was not introduced by definition — it is what falls out of maximizing projected variance under a unit-length constraint. An eigenvector of $C$ is a direction the matrix stretches without rotating. Its corresponding eigenvalue is how much it stretches.\n\nMultiply both sides on the left by $\\mathbf{w}^\\top$:\n\n$$\\mathbf{w}^\\top C \\mathbf{w} = \\lambda \\, \\mathbf{w}^\\top \\mathbf{w} = \\lambda$$\n\nThe variance captured along direction $\\mathbf{w}$ equals the eigenvalue $\\lambda$. To maximize variance, pick the eigenvector with the largest eigenvalue. That is PC1. PC2 uses the eigenvector with the second largest eigenvalue, and so on.\n\nFor the sensor data:\n\n$$C = \\begin{bmatrix} 1.667 & 1.500 \\\\ 1.500 & 1.583 \\end{bmatrix}$$\n\n| Component | Direction           | Eigenvalue ($\\lambda$) | Variance explained |\n| --------- | ------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------ |\n| PC1       | $(0.717,\\; 0.697)$  | $3.126$                | $96.2\\%$           |\n| PC2       | $(0.697,\\; -0.717)$ | $0.124$                | $3.8\\%$            |\n\nThe ratio $3.126 \\,/\\, (3.126 + 0.124)$ gives the 96.2% explained variance from earlier.\n\nKeeping every eigenvector only rotates the coordinate system — no information is lost. Compression starts the moment you drop the smallest eigenvalue directions.\n\n## Optional implementation: NumPy and scikit-learn\n\nThe code below follows the derivation step by step and then cross-checks the result with scikit-learn:\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n\nX = np.array([\n    [2, 1],\n    [3, 2],\n    [4, 2],\n    [5, 4],\n], dtype=float)\n\n# Step 1: Center each feature (mean centering).\nmean = X.mean(axis=0)\nX_centered = X - mean\n\n# Step 2: Build the covariance matrix.\n# This captures both per-feature variance (diagonal)\n# and cross-feature relationships (off-diagonal).\ncovariance = np.cov(X_centered, rowvar=False)\n\n# Step 3: Eigendecompose the covariance matrix.\n# eigh is used instead of eig because C is symmetric —\n# eigh is numerically more stable for symmetric matrices.\neigenvalues, eigenvectors = np.linalg.eigh(covariance)\norder = np.argsort(eigenvalues)[::-1]   # descending by eigenvalue\neigenvalues = eigenvalues[order]\neigenvectors = eigenvectors[:, order]\n\n# Step 4: Keep PC1.\n# The sign of a principal component is arbitrary;\n# flip it so the first weight is positive for readability.\nW = eigenvectors[:, :1]\nif W[0, 0] < 0:\n    W *= -1\n\n# Step 5: Project (compress) and reconstruct.\nZ = X_centered @ W                  # each row is now a single number\nX_reconstructed = Z @ W.T + mean   # back to original space (approximately)\n\nprint(\"PC1 direction:\", W.ravel())\nprint(\"explained variance ratio:\", eigenvalues[0] / eigenvalues.sum())\nprint(\"compressed coordinates:\", Z.ravel())\nprint(\"reconstruction MSE:\", np.mean((X - X_reconstructed) ** 2))\n\n# Verify against scikit-learn.\npca = PCA(n_components=1)\npca.fit(X)\nprint(\"sklearn PC1:\", pca.components_.ravel())\nprint(\"sklearn explained ratio:\", pca.explained_variance_ratio_[0])\n```\n\nThe explained-variance ratio comes out to approximately `0.9617`. The reconstruction MSE is approximately `0.0467` across the eight original values.\n\nThe derivation above used the covariance matrix because it makes the reasoning visible. Production libraries typically compute the same principal subspace using Singular Value Decomposition directly on the centered data matrix, which avoids explicitly building a potentially large covariance matrix and is numerically more stable.\n\n## A final decision checklist\n\nBefore adding PCA to a real pipeline:\n\n1. Are the features correlated and organized around approximately linear directions?\n2. Do the units justify raw variance, or should scaling be learned first?\n3. What do the component weights mean, and does that matter for this use case?\n4. What information lives in the discarded components?\n5. Is the number of components chosen using the real downstream task metric?\n6. Are scaling and PCA fitted only on training data, inside each CV fold?\n7. Does the reduced representation actually outperform the unreduced baseline?\n8. How will reconstruction error and component drift be monitored in production?\n\nPCA is powerful because its promise is narrow. It preserves the strongest linear variation for a chosen number of dimensions. Whether the discarded variation is truly expendable is a decision the algorithm cannot make for you.\n\n## Sources\n- [scikit-learn: PCA](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.decomposition.PCA.html)\n- [scikit-learn: StandardScaler](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.preprocessing.StandardScaler.html)\n- [scikit-learn: Pipelines and composite estimators](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/compose.html)\n";